## Lessons Learned

- VMware Easy Install leaves behind an `autoinst.flp` floppy artifact that must be fully removed — from the `.vmx` file, from VM hardware settings, AND from the NVRAM file. Removing it from only one location is insufficient.
- The `.nvram` file stores EFI/BIOS firmware settings independently of the `.vmx` — deleting it forces VMware to regenerate a clean firmware state on next boot. This is the correct fix when phantom hardware devices persist after `.vmx` cleanup.
- VMware Tools should always be installed immediately after OS installation — it provides the critical drivers for stable guest/host communication. The `tools.remindInstall = "TRUE"` line in a `.vmx` file is a quick way to confirm whether Tools has ever been installed.
- BSOD stop codes that cluster around a specific operation (clipboard copy in this case) point toward the mechanism triggering the crash rather than a random memory error — follow the pattern, not just the code.
- The VMware status bar at the bottom of the VM window displays device connection errors in real time — monitoring it during instability can surface hardware-level issues that don't appear in Windows Event Viewer.
- Two different stop codes appearing in the same instability pattern is an escalation signal — address it immediately rather than waiting to see if it stabilizes on its own.

